West Virginia,Yes,"final grievance decisions must be made within 60 days with 30 day extension permitted absent threat to health, life or safety of litigant",Yes,,Three strikes,limits IFP status; exceptions for imminent danger of serious physical injury; good time credit revocation,NA,,W. Va. Code §§ 25-IAI to 25-IA-8.
Wisconsin,Yes,,Yes,accounts are frozen until full payment; exception for imminent danger of serious physical harm,Three strikes,"limits IFP status; exceptions for imminent danger of serious physical injury; penalties include extension of mandatory release date or eligibility for supervised release, or deprivation of good time",NA,,"Wis. Stat. §§ 301.328, 801.02 (7)(b)(d), 807.15, and 814.29."
Georgia,NA,,Yes,and accounts are frozen until full payment,Three strikes,limits IFP status; exceptions for imminent danger of serious physical injury,NA,,"Ga. Code Ann. § 9-10-14, and § 42-12-1 et seq."
Indiana,NA,,Yes,"case dismissal for failing to pay fees, but courts can authorize fee waivers for exceptional circumstances",Three strikes,limits IFP status; exceptions for immediate danger of serious bodily injury,NA,,Ind. Code Ann. § 33-37-3-3; and Ind. Code § 34-10-1-3.
South Carolina,NA,,Yes,and courts may dismiss cases brought by person who “previously failed to pay filing fees”,Three strikes,"limits IFP status; exceptions for imminent danger of great bodily injury; three strikes penalty of up to one additional year of incarceration; partial or full earned work, education, or good conduct credits revocation for each claim",NA,,"S.C. Code Ann. §§ 24-27-100 to 500 (1996) (""Inmate Litigation Act"" or ""ILA"")."
Nebraska,Yes,and exhaustion in some cases may require pursuing claim through administrative state tort system,NA,,Three strikes,limits IFP status; must obtain court permission to file future claims; exception for danger of serious bodily injury,NA,,"Neb. Rev. Stat. §25-3401. Also see Cole v. Isherwood 271 Neb. 684 (2006) and Cole I, 11 Neb. App. 44 (2002)."
Pennsylvania,NA,,Other,Held unconstitutional by state supreme court (2005),Three strikes,limits IFP status; court may dismiss future claims; exceptions for imminent danger of serious bodily injury,NA,,"42 Pa. Cons. Stat. § 6602 (f). Pennsylvania’s filing fee requirement was found unconstitutional in Payne v. Commonwealth Dep't of Corr., 871 A.2d 795, 807 (Pa. 2005)."
